+# Franchise history to trade follow-through
+
+Verified baseline: v1.22.15/build2230, commit3639a5f51270522c9f6a73971cb52226df343bef, treec251edcf8a6d2a35b157deb916a8cfb7869d6344. Current main and public readiness matched. No related unfinished work was found; the only other open PR was unrelated draft2. Target v1.22.16/build2231 was unused when checked.
+
+## Existing features are reused
+
+HistoricalAssetGraph, its bounded timeline traversal, canonical history registration and the existing historical-assets/player/history routers already support original consideration, subsequent observed movements, exact pick conversion and original-trade links. Current ownership is separate from uninterrupted holding history. Missing clocks/bounds and conflicting ownership stop inferred continuation. Package relationships do not allocate descendant values or imply an individual one-to-one exchange. Current quotes are not historical prices, and later production/championships do not establish decision quality. FOIS remains RETAINED / NOT REVALIDATED.
+
+The bounded Day Traders retained replay contains original trade1333655835015942144, exact pickPICK-2026-R2-ORIG7, selected playerDTOS-P-13346 and later trade1414718106177122304. It is an existing October source replay, supplemented with explicitly disposable pagination waivers. Arkham's retained auction/history is an independent source/isolation control; no unsupported franchise-pick conversion is manufactured for it. These are not newly observed live customer histories.
+
+## Confirmed integration defect and smallest correction
+
+The shared contextual Back URL sanitizer omitted events_page. Real authorized fixture navigation from Franchise History page1 to page2, then a completed trade and Back, returned to page2 but exposed League as the next Back target. The ledger stored page1 and page2 as the same URL and skipped their meaningful distinction. The original matched failure console is retained. Adding the existing read-only pagination parameter to the existing bounded URL allowlist fixes this without changing routes, authentication, history storage, trade calculations or traversal mathematics.
+
+Actual browser controls click History -> trade -> pick -> converted player -> subsequent trade -> original trade. They then use the existing contextual Back through every prior page, including page2 and page1, followed by native Forward and reload. Four viewport sizes320x432,375x667,390x844,1280x900 verify focus, keyboard/touch emulation, readable disclosures and no horizontal overflow. Real account/session middleware and navigation verification endpoints are used. A selected-league change resets the tab's scope rather than replaying the former league. Physical Safari is untested.
+
+## Current-season lifecycle and evidence controls
+
+Fresh owned publication, cache restoration and eviction/reopening are exercised through the actual existing runtime publication/restoration boundary and rendered history/trade routes. Disposable Week1–8 evidence remains Partial; exact current-season packages and later movements remain accessible. Missing chronology preserves original consideration while withholding unsupported continuation. Retained pending/failed trades are verified to exist in the API but cannot open as completed movements. Foreign canonical origins and foreign league/franchise paths are rejected or lose their untrusted origin links. No provider acquisition, FOIS rebuild or new heavyweight work is introduced on rendering.
+
+Original Trade, projection, valuation, scoring, source qualification, exact pick ownership, submitted lineups, saved settings and resource-policy code remain unchanged. The effective2GiB limit, FOIS768MiB and Trade256+128MiB reserves and their lifecycle safeguards remain required. The source and protected-object manifests plus final exact-source canonical/hosted records establish acceptance separately from this source/design note.
+
+## Failure provenance and limitations
+
+Initial probe setup failures omitted the source import path and actual navigation router. Later current-season fixture failures used future event clocks (correctly withheld) and omitted the historical-assets fixture-store binding. A canonical pick API returns string season/round identities; the fixture now asserts that existing contract exactly. Those failures are retained and were corrected in fixture setup, not by changing application admission or evidence rules.
+
+The first baseline JSON snapshot was overwritten by the first corrected-source probe. Its original matched failure console remains intact. A subsequent baseline-source replay, using an independently clean checkout with the same production source tree, supplies a separately labelled snapshot; it is not described as recovered original evidence. Original pre-edit probe variants and failing logs remain separate from final passing evidence.
+
+Local validation scratch recovery removed only an obsolete unused Python3.12 dependency-image backup after recording its checksum/size. The pinned Python3.14.3 validation runtime, all authoritative source, prior logs, artifacts and worktrees remain available. The existing8GiB scratch prerequisite was not reduced. No production infrastructure was changed.
+
+Private authenticated live acceptance is attempted only through an existing authorized surface where available. This session has no owner-browser or Render Shell execution capability; it does not retry the previously rejected external inspection credential. Public readiness/deployment checks are distinct from private history acceptance. No forced production sync, FOIS rebuild, Trade search, cache clear, restart or saved-user-data mutation is part of this task.
